Now, W3.2's `check:` currently "secret scanner; reviewer". Change to "secret scanner (tools/memory_index.py); reviewer" and the `checks` entry: "a scanner over entries/ finds it: `python3 tools/memory_index.py` scans every file under `project-memory/entries/` for the patterns in its `SECRET_PATTERNS` table; a line may be waived with `secret-scan:allow`; the reviewer remains the check for what the patterns do not cover (passwords, tokens with no fixed prefix, personal data)."

Also note: the scanner's patterns themselves live in `tools/memory_index.py`, and gitleaks' own docs contain example keys; our own tool's pattern file would not be under `entries/`, so no self-flagging. Good.

What the check does not establish: it only catches the fixed-prefix formats; a password, a token with no prefix, or personal data passes; the reviewer is still the check for those. Name it.

Why not (b) outside scanner: gitleaks is feature-complete and moving to Betterleaks (research), needs brew/docker/Go (not here, `which` found none); trufflehog makes network calls for live verification; detect-secrets is a pip package and its entropy rules would flag commit hashes in a knowledge repo. All three add an install per project for a MUST that must run on every commit. The kit is stdlib-only (`tools/memory_index.py:17-36`) and travels; a scanner that needs an install is a new dependency every project must satisfy. My #2: rely on what you have. My #8: clumsy speed now (a short regex table) beats skilful later (an install campaign). My #3: whole over broken — the small check plus the reviewer keeps the whole W3.2 rather than leaving it broken.

Why not (c): the reviewer is already the check; changing `check:` to "reviewer" alone would make the MUST honest but weaker, and the hook already runs the tool. The small check costs ~40 lines and catches the common formats. Keep the reviewer as the second check.

